# Line endings.
#
# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true globally, so a plain clone rewrites
# every text file to CRLF on checkout. That is harmless for the markdown, and
# fatal for the scripts: bash reads the trailing CR as part of the interpreter
# path and fails with
#
#     /bin/bash^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# on a machine that has bash perfectly well. This file overrides that per
# type, so the shell scripts and Python land with LF wherever they are cloned.

# Default: let git decide for text, and normalise to LF in the repository.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Anything that runs must keep LF, no matter the platform.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf

# Binaries git must never touch.
*.png   binary
*.zip   binary

# SVG is text and diffs usefully, but keep it LF like everything else.
*.svg   text eol=lf
